CURL(1)                          User Commands                         CURL(1)

NAME
       curl - transfer a URL

SYNOPSIS
       curl [options] [URL...]

DESCRIPTION
       curl is a tool for transferring data from or to a server using URLs.
       It supports HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, and many other protocols.

       curl is powered by libcurl. See libcurl(3) for details.

OPTIONS
   -o, --output <file>
       Write output to <file> instead of stdout.

   -O, --remote-name
       Write output to a local file named after the remote file.

   -L, --location
       Follow redirects. If the server reports that the requested page
       has moved to a different location, this option will make curl
       redo the request on the new location.

   -s, --silent
       Silent or quiet mode. Do not show progress meter or error messages.

   -S, --show-error
       When used with -s, show an error message if it fails.

   -v, --verbose
       Make curl verbose during the operation. Useful for debugging.

   -I, --head
       Fetch the headers only.

   -X, --request <method>
       Specify a custom request method to use when communicating with
       the HTTP server. The specified request method will be used instead
       of the method otherwise used (which defaults to GET).

   -d, --data <data>
       Sends the specified data in a POST request to the HTTP server.

   -H, --header <header>
       Extra header to include in the request when sending HTTP to a server.

   -u, --user <user:password>
       Specify the user name and password to use for server authentication.

   -k, --insecure
       Allow curl to proceed and operate even for server connections
       otherwise considered insecure.

   --cacert <file>
       Use the specified certificate file to verify the peer.

   -A, --user-agent <name>
       Specify the User-Agent string to send to the HTTP server.

   -e, --referer <URL>
       Sends the "Referrer Page" information to the HTTP server.

   --max-time <seconds>
       Maximum time in seconds that you allow the whole operation to take.

   --connect-timeout <seconds>
       Maximum time in seconds that you allow curl to wait for the
       connection to happen.

   --retry <num>
       Retry the request <num> times if a transient error is returned.

   -b, --cookie <data|filename>
       Send the specified data as cookies.

   -c, --cookie-jar <filename>
       Specify to which file you want curl to write all cookies after
       a completed operation.

   --http1.1
       Use HTTP version 1.1.

   --http2
       Use HTTP version 2. (requires nghttp2 support)

   -4, --ipv4
       Resolve names to IPv4 addresses only.

   -6, --ipv6
       Resolve names to IPv6 addresses only.

   -w, --write-out <format>
       Make curl display information on stdout after a completed transfer.

   --compressed
       Request a compressed response and decompress the data.

   -Z, --parallel
       Perform transfers in parallel.

   --version
       Display curl version information and exits.

   --help
       Usage help. This lists all current command line options.

EXAMPLES
       Retrieve a web page:
              curl https://example.com

       Download a file:
              curl -O https://example.com/file.tar.gz

       POST data:
              curl -d "name=value" https://example.com/form

       Follow redirects with progress:
              curl -L -# https://example.com/redirect

       Custom header:
              curl -H "Authorization: Bearer TOKEN" https://api.example.com

FILES
       /etc/ssl/certs/ca-certificates.crt
              Default CA certificate bundle for peer verification.

EXIT CODES
       0      Success.
       1      Unsupported protocol.
       2      Failed to initialize.
       3      URL malformat.
       6      Could not resolve host.
       7      Failed to connect to host.
       22     HTTP page not retrieved (HTTP error 400 or above).
       23     Write error.
       26     Read error.
       28     Operation timed out.
       35     SSL connect error.
       51     The remote peer's SSL certificate was not OK.
       52     The server did not reply anything.
       56     Failure in receiving network data.
       60     SSL peer certificate or SSH remote key was not OK.
       67     Login denied.

